    "" Environment Setup and Dataset Preparation ""

🧩 Environment Setup:
Created a Python virtual environment (.venv) for the project.
Installed and verified TensorFlow, NumPy, Matplotlib, and Jupyter.
Registered the virtual environment as a Jupyter Notebook kernel.

🧩 Library imports:
Imported TensorFlow for building and training the deep-learning model.
Imported NumPy for numerical operations and array handling.
Imported Matplotlib for visualizing handwritten digit images.

🧩 Dataset Loading:
Loaded the MNIST dataset using TensorFlow/Keras.
The dataset contains 70,000 grayscale images of handwritten digits from 0 to 9.
60,000 images are used for training, and 10,000 images are used for testing.
Each image has dimensions of 28 × 28 pixels.

🧩 Understanding variables:
x_train: Training images.
y_train: Correct labels for training images.
x_test: Testing images.
y_test: Correct labels for testing images.

🧩 Image visualization:
Used Matplotlib's imshow() function to display a sample handwritten digit.
Used the cmap (colormap) parameter to control how pixel values are displayed as colors.
cmap='gray' displays the image in grayscale using black, white, and gray shades.
Other colormaps, such as viridis and hot, can also be used for visualization.
Changing the colormap affects only the image's appearance, not the original pixel values or digit label.
Used plt.title() to display the actual digit label and plt.axis('off') to hide the axes.

🧩 Understanding pixel values:
Examined the numerical pixel values of a sample MNIST image using NumPy.
Confirmed that each image has a shape of 28 × 28 pixels, containing 784 pixels.
Observed that pixel intensity values range from 0 to 255.
0 represents black, 255 represents white, and intermediate values represent shades of gray.
Used min() and max() to identify the minimum and maximum pixel values.
Understood that computers represent images as numerical arrays rather than interpreting them visually like humans.
-> Learning Outcome: Learned how handwritten digit images are represented numerically and 
                  how pixel intensities form the basis for image recognition.

🧩 Importance of data normalization:
Normalization scales image pixel values from 0–255 to 0–1.
It provides a consistent range of input values for the CNN model.
It often improves training efficiency and numerical stability.
The original dataset can be preserved by storing normalized values in separate variables.

Note: The digit still looks the same because normalization changes the numerical intensity scale, not the image's underlying shape.

🧩  Reshape images for the CNN: 
reshape() changes the arrangement of the data, not the actual image content.
-1 tells NumPy to calculate the number of images automatically.
28, 28, 1 specifies height, width, and grayscale channel.

why:-
- Reshaped training and testing images to match the input format expected by the CNN model.
- Original image shape: (28, 28); added an extra dimension to represent the grayscale channel.
- Updated shapes: (60000, 28, 28, 1) for training data and (10000, 28, 28, 1) for testing data.
- Used NumPy’s reshape() function without changing the actual image content or pixel values.
- The additional dimension (1) represents the single grayscale channel.

🧩 Building the CNN model:
Created a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) using TensorFlow/Keras.
Added a convolution layer to learn image patterns such as edges and curves.
Used max pooling to reduce feature-map dimensions.
Applied a flatten layer to convert feature maps into a one-dimensional array.
Added dense layers for classification into 10 digit classes (0–9).
Used model.summary() to inspect layer output shapes and model parameters.
Confirmed that the model contains 693,962 trainable parameters.

🧩 Compiling the CNN Model:
Compiled the CNN model using TensorFlow/Keras.
Used the Adam optimizer to update model parameters during training.
Selected sparse categorical cross-entropy as the loss function for classifying digits into 10 classes.
Used accuracy as a metric to monitor model perormance.
Confirmed that the model compiled successfully.
Learning Outcome: Understood how an optimizer, loss function, and evaluation metric prepare a CNN model for training.
🧩 Training the CNN Model:
Trained the CNN model using the MNIST training dataset.
Used five epochs and a batch size of 64.
Reserved 10% of the training data for validation.
Monitored training and validation accuracy and loss during each epoch.
Achieved 99.33% training accuracy and 98.78% validation accuracy after five epochs.
Learning Outcome: Understood how a CNN learns from labeled images and how accuracy and loss help monitor its training performance.

🧩 Model Evaluation:
Evaluated the trained CNN using 10,000 separate MNIST test images.
Used model.evaluate() to calculate test loss and test accuracy.
Achieved a test accuracy of 98.40% and a test loss of approximately 0.0498.
Compared training, validation, and test accuracy to understand model performance on different datasets.
Learning Outcome: Learned how to evaluate a trained CNN on unseen images to measure its ability to recognize handwritten digits.

🧩 Predicting Handwritten Digits:
Used the trained CNN model to predict digits from five test images.
Applied model.predict() to obtain prediction scores for each image.
Used np.argmax() to identify the digit with the highest prediction score.
Compared predicted digits with the actual labels.
All five sample predictions matched their actual labels.
Learning Outcome: Learned how to use a trained CNN model to recognize handwritten digits and verify predictions against actual labels.

🧩 Visualizing Model Predictions(Visualize it as a heatmap):
Visualized five MNIST test images using Matplotlib.
Displayed the predicted digit and actual label above each image.
Used plt.imshow() with cmap='gray' to display the handwritten digits.
Verified that all five predictions matched their actual labels.
Learning Outcome: Learned how to visualize and compare CNN predictions with actual handwritten digit images.

🧩 Visualizing the Confusion Matrix(Visualize it as a heatmap):
Visualized the 10 × 10 confusion matrix using ConfusionMatrixDisplay.
Used a blue color map to distinguish prediction counts.
Observed that diagonal cells contain correct predictions, while off-diagonal cells indicate misclassifications.
Identified digit pairs that the model sometimes confuses.
Confirmed an overall test accuracy of 98.40%.
Learning Outcome: Learned to interpret a confusion matrix heatmap to analyze correct classifications and prediction errors.

🧩 Saving and Loading the Model:
Saved the trained CNN model as handwritten_digit_model.keras.
Loaded the saved model using tf.keras.models.load_model().
Used the loaded model to predict a digit from the test dataset.
Compared the predicted digit with its actual label.
Successfully predicted digit 7 without retraining the model.
Learning Outcome: Learned how to save, reload, and reuse a trained CNN model for handwritten digit recognition.


